kind: perf
upstream: not yet reported to earcut.hpp or earcut.js (both have this index);
  applies after earcut-convex-ring.patch.

findHoleBridge bridges holes in one at a time, scanning a block index whose
blocks each walked their run of the ring from `blockHead` to `blockStop` along
`next`. Every hole spliced into a run lands inside an earlier block's walk, so
the walks lengthen with every hole merged: quadratic in the number of holes
(10,000 random holes walked 2e8 nodes). Each block now records its own K nodes
and walks only those. A merged hole's edges are indexed as blocks of their own,
so every edge is still visited: once, instead of again for every later hole.

Measured 2026-11-01 in the trimkit-dev image (g++ 14.2 -O3, i7-12700K, one
pinned core), best of 7, earcut-convex-ring.patch alone vs with this:
  10,000 holes, 100 x 100 grid   1785 ms  ->  170 ms
  10,000 holes, random cells     9838 ms  ->  537 ms
  10,000 holes, one row          1982 ms  ->  606 ms
  rings without holes            unchanged
Correctness: identical triangles to pristine over 1,425,929 differential fuzz
runs (601 s) and 716 hard inputs, clean under ASan and UBSan. A smaller fix
(splitting a block's run at each splice) is as fast but resolves equal-x ties
differently, so it belongs upstream rather than here.
Covered by: tests/test_earcut.py::test_many_holes_are_not_quadratic (10,000
holes under a time bound), fuzz/earcut.cpp (up to 144 holes).

